"Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1c5547-1ac6-53d1-b475-3c1f3a7a7421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327355} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9bc47f-e4a1-5a48-ad95-9fdc8c30c725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328727} {:answer/id #uuid "e561604b-c400-5b31-b7b4-38788bbd36a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328943} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8db0ff-6f78-5797-8626-bb072f3b6837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329018} {:answer/id #uuid "ef36cb4d-9cd4-585e-b2e1-dfd00cf7e9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1c5547-1ac6-53d1-b475-3c1f3a7a7421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327355} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8241441-5d37-5b80-8d02-80f7b14d12b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be9d95c2-a710-5571-987b-8cd6ddd0d0ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42449" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3+z=0 \\\\ 5y-1+3x=2z+1 \\\\ 3z+2x-1=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+z=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-0+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2854ab92-ed1f-5d7d-be3e-9599ee77142e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327276} {:answer/id #uuid "3e28e4aa-5d1d-5c3f-ada1-e4595bcbe54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327478} {:answer/id #uuid "5aec9977-94b7-52b9-9d5b-6e3ababd8990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327731} {:answer/id #uuid "a344999f-2014-517f-b6ac-76fba5cd2bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328344} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4599c2-f1c9-5ae5-b91d-71e1e423f931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4599c2-f1c9-5ae5-b91d-71e1e423f931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328991} :answer-field/id #uuid "6517ca2d-d648-5470-bdf2-00df6f4aa468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a43a5f7-bf85-5fa1-b02e-c33727064a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42472" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 1 & 19 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 1 & 19 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f950a42d-a540-5921-9a3d-d668bd318fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402830} {:answer/id #uuid "01d7bfc6-a04a-533c-8665-b132159f8eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402831} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6ab905-f6c2-51df-be91-308b16286694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-2{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402832} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1548a5-d455-56f1-be69-1c079b40aef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+4{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402833} {:answer/id #uuid "5a20cf7f-f569-51b2-9f0c-7247587435dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-3{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f950a42d-a540-5921-9a3d-d668bd318fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402830} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2400bde-d5f2-5b5e-81f0-94f5fae33cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abd07e98-c7c9-5953-be97-025190235f0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42562" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{2}:=…$\nII. ${C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-5{C}_{1}$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-5{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166850 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "616b7aa2-2c55-5a29-a908-545a4bad4376" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006b42fc-2958-5c03-bb01-9e0c82e50151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326941} {:answer/id #uuid "0a79e19e-5344-5367-a353-3619d5901912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327026} {:answer/id #uuid "359399f5-a25d-5c9a-9750-19f7ed2e8cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327401} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb40883-4081-52e5-8714-588cbc447c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328286} {:answer/id #uuid "e958f66a-134a-58e6-8820-24bd094afcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359399f5-a25d-5c9a-9750-19f7ed2e8cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327401} :answer-field/id #uuid "e72020b6-67f3-5c05-8801-7f37bc872172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9727bdf0-73b8-53bf-86dc-9b44bfa4400e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42567" :question/problem "In a particular city, about $30%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3672ebc9-24fb-51d7-a9f1-c3eb667bc7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327407} {:answer/id #uuid "681050c1-f90a-52c2-96e6-90b209aad18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327834} {:answer/id #uuid "87400b8d-a49e-54be-98a9-44ee2feebfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328096} {:answer/id #uuid "9be6a1c1-2916-5f20-b765-a456ff73026d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328281} {:answer/id #uuid "c62200c6-6716-501c-bb83-1b638c35509e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87400b8d-a49e-54be-98a9-44ee2feebfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328096} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e8823c0-bb89-5457-a931-f2fd5266cb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "293999e5-5c9a-5b54-b498-f6519c92524e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42579" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} {:answer/id #uuid "43f90f38-cbf5-5d7a-920b-1121b5764f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =0$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327528} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ece4b-a049-56ac-8617-bdfeb30b3311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ee6fb-0023-5a59-b1cc-92b08b1e5bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328478} {:answer/id #uuid "baf8f242-62cb-5b3c-a8e9-9e47509ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce77403f-2bf1-5fb6-9d50-de0552a7684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6563bc-e431-538e-8c1d-8eaa7d0e394f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42657" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c5c7fe-9e8b-501e-8d99-9eaa01e9d9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327291} {:answer/id #uuid "561d0092-52f2-5240-85dd-94406071feea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327678} {:answer/id #uuid "6f77438e-da55-563e-a052-19d551e6520a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327900} {:answer/id #uuid "be6c2167-cf77-5c18-b9a9-c5f9e8dffcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328597} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ea5c82-4696-5794-899e-2fbfedfbd836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1ea5c82-4696-5794-899e-2fbfedfbd836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328628} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f8ee01-9c68-5cfd-a230-414249ca8bcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c824c20-e7d8-5ee0-a1d9-a9f4762eb921" :question/math-academy-id "q-42662" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2f149fb4f32fb264e0af6898e7737d570ec90bbff31cf1256eb9bd2636ceab3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/58/589f9f524a7c98da61ad6c5001b8d392d89073d5734cd70cfa750d153fe6f7b7.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=-2v.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=4|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-4u.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =-2v-4u \\\\  & =-4u-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-4u-2v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110574 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09120532-94e0-544e-82f2-1bf9bff66e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327007} {:answer/id #uuid "1e42e95f-712b-5a56-a15d-715693430cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327195} {:answer/id #uuid "4f41c3c2-7d73-57c0-a776-77a9f803c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327623} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd3b79c-f5bc-566d-8e25-ec90fec7b98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327750} {:answer/id #uuid "83cb8e51-60d2-590e-b195-be701bac2c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f41c3c2-7d73-57c0-a776-77a9f803c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5dee8f8-0eb9-56f7-9b8b-ea4b26fd25fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66851f3f-81cd-56a2-896f-bd1b46d94ca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42663" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the value $3\\pi $ is not the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3b19d7-9caa-5865-b36b-62dba7ca1b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327054} {:answer/id #uuid "12bc31bd-410e-5cec-b3e8-db5ec16e6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327095} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8032c3-8159-562d-b85e-ce01f3a9419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd9827b-fe26-5576-843d-788fca86322f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328732} {:answer/id #uuid "f6116bad-8f2c-5913-918c-8429cac9d1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8032c3-8159-562d-b85e-ce01f3a9419c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327189} :answer-field/id #uuid "624e21c0-b503-5eed-93a7-5e549f2e0d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfe5446c-2b26-5e48-974b-de364027f648" :question/math-academy-id "q-42669" :question/problem "![](images/04/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8297713-fb86-59f2-af4d-850af2575541" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Non-Uniform Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "769c296e-be54-5773-908f-c2313dfd929b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327949} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3a1a91-f16d-52d9-aa64-00eb0eafa7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328027} {:answer/id #uuid "cffe0ad1-83ac-5cd4-8c47-9af48c953ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328763} {:answer/id #uuid "de616544-4d9c-5ba7-9211-c04453349876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328882} {:answer/id #uuid "fd27d444-2839-5b26-9af5-3b3bf6d560d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f3a1a91-f16d-52d9-aa64-00eb0eafa7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328027} :answer-field/id #uuid "0abbc756-c503-5905-984d-12f4344f3c3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9734bee1-71b3-5340-a6c8-32c60363a7cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-42728" :question/problem "The rolls of an unfair six-sided die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{8})+2(\\frac{1}{6})+3(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{6})+5(\\frac{1}{8})+6(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{2}{6}+\\frac{3}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{24}+\\frac{8}{24}+\\frac{12}{24}+\\frac{16}{24}+\\frac{15}{24}+\\frac{36}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+8+12+16+15+36}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c3fc090-22e5-5f05-9634-b8c645cf5700" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value Given the Frequency of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ce0594-ae9a-5533-b4e8-9b16a4434e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370948} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2d678f-d252-5400-be5e-828422888057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{70}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370949} {:answer/id #uuid "39a17018-6a1b-5fa0-a4b2-d57693172562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370950} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8d0e30-5870-5882-90fe-bbe6d3d8ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{110}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370951} {:answer/id #uuid "11bce5a2-21ae-5099-bf11-e90bee429d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b8d0e30-5870-5882-90fe-bbe6d3d8ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{110}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370951} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d39d50-e814-58b3-b920-cbbb549d00fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b33e7f8-97bd-52b2-9fc5-eb73c2b2d7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-42732" :question/problem "A bag contains $4$ five-dollar bills, $8$ ten-dollar bills, and $6$ twenty-dollar bills. If one bill is randomly drawn from a bag, what is its expected value?" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value of a randomly drawn bill. Then the support is $S={5,10,20}.$\n\nIn the bag there are  $4$ five-dollar bills, $8$ ten-dollar bills and $6$ twenty-dollar bills (eighteen bills in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =\\frac{4}{18}=\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ P(10) & =\\frac{8}{18}=\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ P(20) & =\\frac{6}{18}=\\frac{3}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10)+20\\cdot f(20) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{2}{9})+10(\\frac{4}{9})+20(\\frac{3}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}+\\frac{40}{9}+\\frac{60}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+40+60}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2affdaad-96aa-5484-a442-927e85d5bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327314} {:answer/id #uuid "3521707d-b15a-5eb1-a674-60c3a068dbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327393} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebe1f6b-8a8a-56c5-a9de-f051b92a33f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328176} {:answer/id #uuid "b9883cba-3829-5eae-b3f0-46c20405acfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328552} {:answer/id #uuid "f84442ad-34a5-5352-9847-b2355864c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84442ad-34a5-5352-9847-b2355864c670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329110} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b837f60-46c5-55db-be33-a2ded8972ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d1cd618-3e37-5bc5-a865-78591e0eea79" :question/math-academy-id "q-42788" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘})=2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=1\\,{120}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘}={60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{∘}={300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}=-{20}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{4}=-{20}^{∘}+{300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}={40}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{4}={280}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x={160}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x=1\\,{120}^{∘}+1\\,{440}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=160.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12816130-b7ae-58a5-9f0f-13cc67f21c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327091} {:answer/id #uuid "6184fdd6-eb33-584c-a47e-2dced8a8cf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327786} {:answer/id #uuid "907e306a-a246-574c-a744-8bdd110c28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328195} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc6b643-f6b1-53f2-993d-a1106fb48532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328288} {:answer/id #uuid "f59c64f1-4d1a-5a50-80ef-df0df82a9e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "907e306a-a246-574c-a744-8bdd110c28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328195} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d377a9e-b5c4-59ea-9955-f41f6b988ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd51b773-7114-5c60-8ae3-bd4c43f5e27a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42789" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x=-2\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+n\\pi \\,\\,\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ea83062-5744-52ef-9b3e-f9805ed092fb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355f2d5d-2f1c-5183-9fc4-940e1d62b9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327398} {:answer/id #uuid "5a18aedc-b98f-5616-95a6-211a5ed885f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327725} {:answer/id #uuid "5e141f5d-18c0-5c23-bef3-f0a6b9aa1a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327763} {:answer/id #uuid "7da3a5ce-9c8e-580e-81aa-cc187abf86f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328019} {:answer/id #uuid "c8868cab-79ad-5d64-b43d-dedd294b0996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a18aedc-b98f-5616-95a6-211a5ed885f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327725} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe60e29-6dc6-56ce-98e0-280765400fee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14202e95-52cc-54cc-94ac-037a05937a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42790" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric  equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+6\\pi n,\\,x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\,\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi , & \\,\\frac{x}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+6n\\pi , & \\,x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+6n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63284434-4b3a-5036-9d63-2797b427e386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327799} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0ff12a-aa79-5531-934f-fc7d87ba123e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327896} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3e768c-d56c-5ca2-bd60-c5b2fa8873b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328152} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d19493-9167-5572-b666-0ecf3d9de0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328545} {:answer/id #uuid "ef38a8cd-a821-545c-a71e-6f832f2ebcd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63284434-4b3a-5036-9d63-2797b427e386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327799} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1f91c73-801b-5d0e-8fcf-e4a0d73b513d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35e600df-b2a1-5526-a22d-d7aabd8d69d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42876" :question/problem "Given that $A=3I$, where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=108$\n- $\\text{det}(A)-6\\,\\text{det}(I)=0$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(A)=54$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=27\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=4\\cdot 27=108\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-6\\,\\text{det}(I)=27-6\\cdot 1=21\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(A)=27+27=54\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84e45ca-c250-5e65-a04e-4714a485f38e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Determinant of a Triangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fab934-2670-534b-bc7c-5b4ab1839980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327261} {:answer/id #uuid "9efe610f-b3c6-59d8-993d-7efc7b602b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328308} {:answer/id #uuid "a12ea6fb-b999-5a3e-afff-5d91412cad98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328334} {:answer/id #uuid "eaba2705-06de-56c7-bdef-94c73f7b77d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328988} {:answer/id #uuid "ed692d99-8b34-5d8c-8a57-4b9b07196026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a12ea6fb-b999-5a3e-afff-5d91412cad98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328334} :answer-field/id #uuid "007ced75-a4aa-5f10-91c8-7507f179426f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8faf33fc-c569-57de-9ee0-228c7beea0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42885" :question/problem "Consider the matrices $A$ and $B$ given below. Find $det(A)-det({B}^{T}).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2.5 & 0.21 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ det(B) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2.5 & 0.21 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $det({B}^{T})=det(B)=-6.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(A)-det({B}^{T})=2-(-6)=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a3b4306-55a9-5f15-98c8-f5d60b6a40ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327443} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf0eaf4-db08-533b-aed6-515990875a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327466} {:answer/id #uuid "72c7c4d8-6511-5726-b3e9-0656b209f8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327928} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc077c-9a61-5c22-a6a7-607c04c3efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200} {:answer/id #uuid "95047154-213d-51aa-9ff0-8ef74040020d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fc077c-9a61-5c22-a6a7-607c04c3efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328200} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a9794c-8ea9-5381-b6f9-0d109bd0dcbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3923a242-a394-5f37-9f6d-741dafd435c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-42886" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n- $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec⁡x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0384fc6a-338f-52f3-9912-45dc7776f831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326970} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee7c280-f909-5ec8-8c9c-c74ada42fe15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327894} {:answer/id #uuid "946e20b5-0492-5fd6-ae6e-147fd17c870c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328230} {:answer/id #uuid "a7fe3ae2-cc9e-5511-a146-54bd7d489b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328391} {:answer/id #uuid "e858b1fe-b1e0-50be-bed8-8e186555a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "946e20b5-0492-5fd6-ae6e-147fd17c870c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328230} :answer-field/id #uuid "733bce34-68ed-5d19-8098-0ba365d5ad91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3344b843-2ccb-5b01-90af-e0b77e74221e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42890" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{12}=2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(4-3) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a941c4e1-5e36-53ce-bc6b-7d6663d0963c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C_{1}:=C_{1} + 4C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2} - 3C_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160629} {:answer/id #uuid "f458abd4-da18-5702-90a4-330e62adc6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C_{1}:=C_{1} - 3C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2} + C_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160630} {:answer/id #uuid "1dabc275-1f78-58e5-af0a-2f8732ba71e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C_{1}:=C_{1} - 3C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2} + 4C_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160631} {:answer/id #uuid "34741622-1ae3-5eb4-ab55-84a7650c2729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C_{1}:=C_{1} - 3C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2} - 4C_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160632} {:answer/id #uuid "5536cf5f-999a-5c38-9842-8c8dc2cc8268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C_{1}:=C_{1} - 4C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2} - 3C_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160633}] :answer-field/id #uuid "516f55fe-7fee-5a67-ba34-beb24397ddb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae629206-9379-5164-8cd8-8b7de2d1dd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+4{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402849} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7b06ad-0e94-585e-8310-368aee37f960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402850} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d041e9-b045-57f0-802c-3e06f948a087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402851} {:answer/id #uuid "87a20a0c-83fd-540e-aa98-9006f1c7cbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-4{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-3{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402852} {:answer/id #uuid "650c8674-8a72-5292-8d19-40ec5f80cf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650c8674-8a72-5292-8d19-40ec5f80cf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402853} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad6392da-77cb-57bf-a513-c82c1f289bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a68c2e7c-c605-5187-a1fa-13349acf01b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-42917" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7000a217-4435-526b-b200-effd548ffb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327908} {:answer/id #uuid "99a6dd33-35c1-56e8-be21-39631ff1b887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328261} {:answer/id #uuid "b44e7329-de37-5ec8-82e6-18783ad06a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328502} {:answer/id #uuid "c418b73b-c4e0-5117-9c26-b479706f4607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328654} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4d300-ace5-5df3-9e76-83e1460f7723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44e7329-de37-5ec8-82e6-18783ad06a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328502} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5a31b6-8c92-56e7-b3da-d9146c997969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea69780-9815-5bcf-b663-589f1bb5bcec" :question/math-academy-id "q-42956" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$K=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(K) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(K) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2-0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ae712d-ae5f-58af-9a06-7d502aab05e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327251} {:answer/id #uuid "4303fa99-3020-53e1-bcf0-2f27b0ba4725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327519} {:answer/id #uuid "56410e2b-e1a0-5749-89fd-02655135b930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327680} {:answer/id #uuid "be4b9cd2-d705-5fb5-8f81-0ae81f728a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328595} {:answer/id #uuid "c834bbe7-5ef8-5898-bc76-0da6bd0a2d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be4b9cd2-d705-5fb5-8f81-0ae81f728a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328595} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a6b9dc-f91c-5eea-ae4a-4d84007e5f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90c835e0-83aa-519b-9995-715569dd5d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-43000" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d65bd9-9697-55d7-81da-edd05a273db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327374} {:answer/id #uuid "a9819235-6a71-54a1-bc72-06895dbab668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328402} {:answer/id #uuid "bc75b843-6b44-5d5c-a07e-1868901cb4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328573} {:answer/id #uuid "cd786d05-83c9-59ec-82fd-6fe4800b1cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328740} {:answer/id #uuid "d630ddbc-a7a6-565a-90a1-142145c0718a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc75b843-6b44-5d5c-a07e-1868901cb4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328573} :answer-field/id #uuid "268c6e9e-42e1-596d-a6dd-db0f3ac79607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccc604d3-9ad8-5639-a449-b6958ef153b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43078" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(X)=105.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}$ (switching the first and the third rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(105) \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee277f-7d39-51d6-ae8e-804455cc5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "9908e0fd-b391-508e-8dd9-9f1b7572eaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328256} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a22e-59a5-554f-b4d6-d662134d1479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328503} {:answer/id #uuid "d0528873-0750-5fae-828d-e2dd5c23f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a085cb5f-e013-5736-9ee5-2fef11903a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770be8a8-209f-5a4a-8f5b-267370ed95fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43288" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "141d61c7-1d82-52fe-b977-19daa910da9b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43371a21-e3c4-54a0-9206-f820869cdf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327520} {:answer/id #uuid "5b837a3e-7895-581c-9547-40fad30c41d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327738} {:answer/id #uuid "7323b252-fad1-5e66-a205-f98c81464280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327932} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d1151f-bdbb-5b1c-9ec7-0cc63db29b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328329} {:answer/id #uuid "f467c0f8-8dd0-5354-a064-9d4356dc82bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43371a21-e3c4-54a0-9206-f820869cdf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327520} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d3df98-9ad8-500d-858a-a65793ee1efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52f9b951-61b8-5687-9876-13fa3e1021d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-43292" :question/problem "${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={tan}^{4}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={tan}^{4}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bc8c875-3393-5fd6-90cf-87737e16f2f2" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d51a21-90c5-5b54-a6e4-e0b0b8a2ed02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327134} {:answer/id #uuid "40320fe3-c500-5dcb-a7c9-96f211e3f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327498} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98372a-b734-5075-bb5b-a84d747704c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} {:answer/id #uuid "95f2320a-4032-50dc-aeeb-456b3b00cb5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328238} {:answer/id #uuid "ace65715-85a6-5e2d-ba3b-d82ca77a9505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40320fe3-c500-5dcb-a7c9-96f211e3f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327498} :answer-field/id #uuid "34fb1090-54c7-5d88-a697-5bc7387e677b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db749ad3-a154-5aac-9219-0c39bfac704c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43298" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e85a2d68-d49f-5bf4-ab62-13ad8c35b0d4" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expression Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9790e4-77cb-5ff4-ac43-39c78331e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327191} {:answer/id #uuid "9939c042-c8aa-5188-bbb4-1c3c3f7a7f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328257} {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f911ba-6e39-5c15-993b-525d98cd4ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328397} {:answer/id #uuid "e51dd51b-095f-57d7-a137-944306de7d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a407281c-48ae-50b3-a5ba-d986468525b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328354} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d7ae64-5756-5393-953b-b3fac5aef0ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eb8e400-2919-528a-91cb-5d1221ea0b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-43308" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "First, use the identity $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the parentheses in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55cfe684-dc18-5ab1-b164-6d50fade618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327676} {:answer/id #uuid "8d23ee9d-454e-5907-a98d-d759e11996ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328151} {:answer/id #uuid "b7359851-720e-5ab4-876a-6265d25f515d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328528} {:answer/id #uuid "cf531d87-8a79-5162-b024-e25dc07d7e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} {:answer/id #uuid "e7164a97-e526-5ef2-8ced-7e38b414e248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf531d87-8a79-5162-b024-e25dc07d7e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc9b871-5875-5e5c-8a91-d0aec6bf5882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55d7cd50-6834-55b6-a52f-4f6d251b49dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-43339" :question/problem "![](images/85/85b13f2236c5f00da34ea71fdbfc967882fa6e66c347f6ef40c0604391a5d68e.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $15{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $m∠A.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 15 & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15}{30} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129122 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f720e8d-bb1b-59ca-9ab6-dc161291bf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327071} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc7ca04-3dfc-5466-a866-a199437da7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327775} {:answer/id #uuid "7851f496-18f4-5ce9-b27a-0a6147c6176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327963} {:answer/id #uuid "85974f52-6385-50ae-a81b-e8bc2b19d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328085} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb14292-bdff-5641-bc24-4dffec7b4e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7851f496-18f4-5ce9-b27a-0a6147c6176e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327963} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a5f161-27b6-5dd4-aa10-c1cb6123c7a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72d5418e-aa83-5e26-b56d-9365871c6a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43411" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x+{90}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=8.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110553 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03491655-9b81-5fc0-bf20-e4f74a9c72ad" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3181638b-4d66-589a-b061-04d74bf07e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327372} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd3b151-608d-541d-b9c4-1224e1328c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327630} {:answer/id #uuid "584096c0-c59b-5b5c-802a-e72a377ffdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327708} {:answer/id #uuid "5b36cc0f-2f9f-59d1-bc2e-d088509a383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327734} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4c20ff-9024-5eb1-89f9-dc0efac40673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd3b151-608d-541d-b9c4-1224e1328c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327630} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac4f7c2-82a2-5f28-8ff9-2ad956252386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a581c5d1-c937-510f-8cbd-8478ab152d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-43617" :question/problem "$\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110585 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "704dd449-03cc-5cb5-8d71-9633864418fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/901cc06ce7661348fa18bb172f0a014e0c0d49e6f0a788cfe5926faa6e3061b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392556} {:answer/id #uuid "ab422978-41b5-5448-9e5c-293965809dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2939d0005167bb5771d7a566af50eff3c0d595ff95666d97d0d52597518f814.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392557} {:answer/id #uuid "537f4564-f5f1-5ad4-9cdb-0876e35602eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392558} {:answer/id #uuid "3969e61c-1862-53e5-8d39-b6a5cccbc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7aadddedfe33accf816c81268b8873d02a324034e6ab26bdbfa9895989e7e63e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392559} {:answer/id #uuid "99f07ab8-6830-58b2-8709-6db297f0d693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7aff749f5804fb774a334918b62ff9f20fd8e384fcebbc372e811ce51729d4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537f4564-f5f1-5ad4-9cdb-0876e35602eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392558} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b945ad-4e83-57d3-875d-408d14586c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e78d2563-d22e-5b86-b89f-f6aab65d4ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43659" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e922a42-6deb-5bc6-a4f2-a8d06f3fea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328024} {:answer/id #uuid "d59f7611-aece-5245-9299-86b545be9496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0e28cc-1799-536e-bd55-0a06dc16bb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328867} {:answer/id #uuid "de5640a4-5333-5d9b-b991-e89e51fe95ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328881} {:answer/id #uuid "ed73643a-713f-5f7a-aea6-c63401f50949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d59f7611-aece-5245-9299-86b545be9496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2512d590-4b14-5e67-bd6c-bb6c4cebbb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e2ce70-a218-5096-a57b-5f93338562ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-44337" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aa8a38-9ecc-5e87-9899-fa2c52620615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326982} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0d4423-cdbd-59bc-9519-c02229ca242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327296} {:answer/id #uuid "842c8b78-fb24-5dfb-9556-59ef1eff7a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328076} {:answer/id #uuid "8f307607-46e6-546c-b68f-66ba5c7a0d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{5}x+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328184} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b36271-f849-5b31-8979-398e7e127d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04aa8a38-9ecc-5e87-9899-fa2c52620615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326982} :answer-field/id #uuid "637a8d42-8d66-59a6-9b11-fb936c324619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8ebc497-48a2-5b22-a14f-221db06dd5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-44404" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+5y=10?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+5y=10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y & =10 \\\\ 5y & =-x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=5x-4$ has a slope of $5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot 5=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=5x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5cea50-fecb-5d5b-8a89-f4f34d327f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328005} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3ca05e-2e03-5005-8439-1e9b0e51d16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d0efcf-7b5d-51ae-a082-657d77e63c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328768} {:answer/id #uuid "df927e48-fbac-512c-b73b-41214fbca6fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328892} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e47ca3-6ef9-5cec-9235-9aa609798010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f3ca05e-2e03-5005-8439-1e9b0e51d16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4084a6-ffaf-52c0-9e02-59ab5e2b3839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa337919-23eb-5736-a04b-9af0d92af77d" :question/math-academy-id "q-44407" :question/problem "The lines $4x-8y=7$ and $3y-px=9$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $4x-8y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8y & =7 \\\\ -8y & =-4x+7 \\\\ 8y & =4x-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $3y-px=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-px & =9 \\\\ 3y & =px+9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{p}{3}x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $4x-8y=7$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $3y-px=9$ is $\\frac{p}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{p}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,p=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c942d3-63a4-59a3-b6d0-9aa7ddba1439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327087} {:answer/id #uuid "49403a97-405f-5f84-96e2-5b992bd4eab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327572} {:answer/id #uuid "748c09d4-9a5f-51d6-ba16-a446207f2ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327937} {:answer/id #uuid "74a46401-9b4e-5abc-9a97-e45d5b010ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327938} {:answer/id #uuid "fc47eef6-da40-5792-aae8-9d2421e2e586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49403a97-405f-5f84-96e2-5b992bd4eab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327572} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7213bfa-2e48-5cbc-80d5-fc5e4597837b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43671ef1-4b81-53b4-8687-766126e69d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-44625" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-5x>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x & >0 \\\\ x(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ \nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-5x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156bcb67-c5ab-5a9d-9cd9-5ddaf9376251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327120} {:answer/id #uuid "2411b1e9-12ad-5b81-ba70-ccb8cf3693f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327248} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad20e07-82a6-5edb-8105-373f2bea1d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327448} {:answer/id #uuid "438c3f30-ecc4-5f1d-b541-2dc21275ee52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327521} {:answer/id #uuid "4b018e8c-71a0-58f6-be65-e7b784b88781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156bcb67-c5ab-5a9d-9cd9-5ddaf9376251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327120} :answer-field/id #uuid "75bacceb-193c-56fc-ba0a-3973ddc2eaca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999c69b4-b8e1-5935-8b29-a7c23a4a8300" :question/math-academy-id "q-4463" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+4}-2}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+4-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110602 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a5593e-d6ed-5927-b3a1-d1daf628097e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327122} {:answer/id #uuid "30c1bcff-f9d0-5c30-91f5-4ef435a4a0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327366} {:answer/id #uuid "76680e20-3119-50e8-b90e-149dee6da095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327947} {:answer/id #uuid "9aeb19e0-e051-568b-87f4-243574507bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328276} {:answer/id #uuid "b5707a21-7927-57dc-8dfb-375cdb25c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a5593e-d6ed-5927-b3a1-d1daf628097e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327122} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cd0532-96ff-5c54-9e68-ab598a48b1e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30c4eaa9-084c-53b2-8078-d461a7041d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-44691" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,-9)$ and is perpendicular to $y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, a perpendicular line would need to be vertical. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $x=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(-7,-9),$ which has an $x$-coordinate of $-7.$ So, the perpendicular line is $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b06f58f-7bb2-5bd1-8278-f6381faec253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327167} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6f00af-1911-5327-b3e6-43ed2b1e37bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327171} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb67c8e-c11d-5480-901a-a4dc56ba68e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327201} {:answer/id #uuid "a682458c-8f09-5bf5-830a-3eb04defdf3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328376} {:answer/id #uuid "b6957e91-9a8b-5836-9a9f-2b9c85abb341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb67c8e-c11d-5480-901a-a4dc56ba68e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327201} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5172847-c0c1-5695-a4d4-d62431eb3cbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "382234f6-a36f-52d1-a86a-8c336acdbd1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-4471" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-1}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-\\sqrt{x})(x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3ff37f0-6e45-5da4-ac87-1b93c8df3e80" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Solutions of Compound Inequalities on Number Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2430143-e3ef-56c0-9f1f-964a44b1fe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ec8574742229a2c7fd5af8920f9137e2b25e28a6f02a7cf1edcc8a4db4b6d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392570} {:answer/id #uuid "42638bec-e49e-5814-a427-d1911ca95c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a3b83780bbd15328077dc45eef965aaf311e704990ee3cb45078d76c96183a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392571} {:answer/id #uuid "304db39b-9fd8-57ff-a43e-494c6882a179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8c019b0d7a2b34e9791394ce1ac50f8db22b1d7fb9296a8c119a80a583d63ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392572} {:answer/id #uuid "2102c409-a8fd-5bbe-a29f-a0866257c255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3ff8ace8f19f48631b57968c7aad223613e65f5be6f67a3196bd5fa9158ae51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392573} {:answer/id #uuid "95f7974a-4774-510c-92d0-7a4e59b60df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a8e25a0a97ec193574fd8099e2dfdce70ed887e9f527b855b1ca0957ae7885b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2430143-e3ef-56c0-9f1f-964a44b1fe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ec8574742229a2c7fd5af8920f9137e2b25e28a6f02a7cf1edcc8a4db4b6d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392570} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe31165c-2ea1-57a8-ac77-e3f03a131397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e996239f-cafe-55a4-9e1e-af5edfdca48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-44761" :question/problem "Which of the following number lines gives the solution to $-1<-5x-6<4?$" :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <-5x-6<4 \\\\ -1+6 & <-5x-6+6<4+6 \\\\ 5 & <-5x<10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-5.$ However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the signs of the inequalities.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & <-5x<10 \\\\ \\frac{5}{-5} & >\\frac{-5x}{-5}>\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ -1 & >x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $-1>x>-2$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: \n\n$$\n-2<x<-1\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n![](images/91/91ec8574742229a2c7fd5af8920f9137e2b25e28a6f02a7cf1edcc8a4db4b6d5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1479d18e-80dd-5a14-92c9-4c2003632351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327109} {:answer/id #uuid "4afcb994-3702-574a-9c04-5ae245b062df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327588} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5a904f-538c-5306-b3ca-8b8751c552b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327992} {:answer/id #uuid "861d866f-753f-50bd-9c15-a314626f03e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328092} {:answer/id #uuid "cc05aa1f-4c51-552f-8ebd-fe4ed0bbee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "861d866f-753f-50bd-9c15-a314626f03e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328092} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff99e160-46c8-5df3-a4f3-b91ec24011a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29870ddd-41f6-5225-8553-678aca9119e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-4553" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=2x$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{u}^{3}-u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)\\cdot \\sqrt{(2x)^{3}-(2x)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${f}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2\\sqrt{8(2)^{3}-2(2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{64-4} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c0dc5d-22fa-56ae-ab34-3b3ce6e831a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327944} {:answer/id #uuid "843b6944-d842-5308-bff2-694eb6f2f194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2-\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328077} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd3e1b1-f310-5d10-9128-61a9ef1f9114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328146} {:answer/id #uuid "a6adcac9-a082-570d-b7b9-84c64cf73cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328379} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a654dd-a6e5-5fc2-a98d-2d321b19e2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-\\sqrt{x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75c0dc5d-22fa-56ae-ab34-3b3ce6e831a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327944} :answer-field/id #uuid "da648259-9815-5bd1-b05c-fb2f22213af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3da59ba-9fd7-5e38-87bc-bd24039d3bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-4555" :question/problem "For $x>2$, find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eac988ba-ea97-52c7-90a7-d451ee7f49e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals on Which a Rational Function Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184cb986-356b-5b99-a26d-3c28037cbf9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327143} {:answer/id #uuid "229a8b7a-fb69-51c8-9752-e403414ceb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327233} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9f6c3f-8609-5e1f-ae1c-e5a050990595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327596} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae44e58-9700-5cdf-bf75-0cbc7d570e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327991} {:answer/id #uuid "efcfb790-d29a-5a46-b95a-2182bff9ba84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9f6c3f-8609-5e1f-ae1c-e5a050990595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327596} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a29085-f6a5-57a6-b07d-857f7c485ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abf06e7d-1262-50fe-8aac-916db45605bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-4561" :question/problem "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100994 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e9df1ae-8176-555b-987d-94773c27d435" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane Using the Distance Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f69a451-fc1a-53c3-99ba-8794adb192de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327206} {:answer/id #uuid "336a30a2-8421-5c3f-9236-26d06f04d9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327378} {:answer/id #uuid "339042f1-7a53-52e5-9e12-f3fd477136df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327381} {:answer/id #uuid "5620faab-8634-5c06-8781-d34feae4212a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327679} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b816b2-1418-5f58-be00-510d1833ac0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339042f1-7a53-52e5-9e12-f3fd477136df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327381} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cc9205-adbc-534f-bd1f-78044226c4d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1784a5ba-0bb5-56cc-a185-96af988fd7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-45759" :question/problem "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(4,-5)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Find the radius of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-0)}^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{d}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0697fb32-13a8-5152-929c-1cbc92697a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326990} {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59d2b0-87ec-535c-98db-46f721956829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327771} {:answer/id #uuid "875b0e21-3bdb-537e-83a7-6070520501cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328098} {:answer/id #uuid "8c78c851-07e7-51bc-a294-83ea44fc4afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6067e08a-a253-5424-8dcb-5049856ea945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5295f76-3e41-52a4-88d5-2769f2995c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-45875" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1351ff-6581-5cc5-90e7-dd06f6e8c14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327297} {:answer/id #uuid "80b6fe9e-0506-5dda-971f-dc0d61ec4a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3+5\\text{i}m)(3-5\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328042} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7c8efa-e9ed-5427-9384-353a1c64efa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328157} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc57fa3-bb89-5a7a-bd30-97bbd6466a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5(3+4\\text{i}m)(3-4\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328287} {:answer/id #uuid "d3280bbc-cdd4-5168-adda-2abb101ed90e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5(4+3\\text{i}m)(4-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a1351ff-6581-5cc5-90e7-dd06f6e8c14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327297} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c8abfda-8c32-53b8-bdc8-9bf64abd97ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54d3d1e2-2e59-5152-a868-718f646fc4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-46507" :question/problem "The expression $-100-36{m}^{2}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out $-4$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-100-36{m}^{2}=-4(25+9{m}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(25+9{m}^{2}).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25+9{m}^{2} & =25-(-9{m}^{2}) \\\\  & =25-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}{m}^{2}) \\\\  & ={5}^{2}-{(3\\text{i}m)}^{2} \\\\  & =(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into the expression, we get\n\n$$\n-4(25+9{m}^{2})=-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2478305-485c-53a6-9d6c-a72e0764dd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given Two Real Roots and a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d53f205-3f13-5653-97f7-16a13fccb6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327333} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0027e4-9313-5ed2-941a-ed87cac9171d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}+100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327346} {:answer/id #uuid "97aead54-9510-5fc5-aa9f-eeb7b75426e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328249} {:answer/id #uuid "bb42db12-58ef-5359-a1c1-28a86be9c910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328567} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0f7336-c824-5328-b9af-5399374350f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb42db12-58ef-5359-a1c1-28a86be9c910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{2}-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328567} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7458a1-7227-5719-a279-7114bb1314ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c80b75c-c4ac-520f-a68d-126d07fda083" :question/math-academy-id "q-46582" :question/problem "The quadratic polynomial $P(t)$ has roots ${t}_{1}=-5,{t}_{2}=5,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $4.$ Find $P(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${t}_{1}=-5$ and ${t}_{2}=5,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t-{t}_{1})(t-{t}_{2}) & =(4)(t-(-5))(t-5) \\\\  & =4(t+5)(t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(t+5)(t-5) & =4({t}^{2}-25) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(t)=4{t}^{2}-100.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0262c8-c029-5a72-846b-1b4f4f3306ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given a Double Root and a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e9cc2a-2e40-5eac-8225-9a3647af4013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-18y-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326953} {:answer/id #uuid "397d37fa-9c19-5957-8545-f3e9c8b2907e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-18y+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327435} {:answer/id #uuid "6c86f783-35aa-5013-881b-97dcb72fefca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-9y-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327870} {:answer/id #uuid "bdba8a19-4868-5b73-b78a-b2f2642ee9f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-9y+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328591} {:answer/id #uuid "d9689658-04a0-54b7-9816-4e531d6a63dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-24y+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397d37fa-9c19-5957-8545-f3e9c8b2907e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}-18y+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327435} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d4f3164-f7c0-5641-841e-102bb0a49284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6f211f1-c9b6-5172-aa3d-db8224b3a9c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-46588" :question/problem "The quadratic polynomial $P(y)$ has the double root ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3.$ Find $P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(y-{y}_{1})^{2} & =3{(y-3)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(y-3)}^{2} & =3({y}^{2}-6y+9) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}-18y+27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(y)=3{y}^{2}-18y+27.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70c149d8-3739-564c-97c5-6636860ad0c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given Two Imaginary or Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0022c81d-7249-57ea-b85c-faca52fa725e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{z}^{2}-12z-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326934} {:answer/id #uuid "1c62967a-35b8-51ec-b618-768b175287e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{z}^{2}+12z-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327179} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0e210e-e687-5df2-8531-30a91aae299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{z}^{2}+6z-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327485} {:answer/id #uuid "80f269b1-70e4-5874-ac0d-1c99549a7558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{z}^{2}-6z+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328046} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf7a053-7d48-5047-9768-06a394556fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{z}^{2}+12z-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c62967a-35b8-51ec-b618-768b175287e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{z}^{2}+12z-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327179} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ac5061-3c66-50a0-9298-0fb8d6af5746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99f2b811-f0ad-5df2-9c8e-3f3014230013" :question/math-academy-id "q-46664" :question/problem "The quadratic polynomial $f(z)$ has real coefficients, has roots ${z}_{1}=3+2\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=3-2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $-2.$ Find $f(z).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=3+2\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=3-2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=-2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) & =(-2)(z-(3+2\\text{i}))(z-(3-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)(z-(3+2\\text{i}))(z-(3-2\\text{i})) & =(-2)({z}^{2}-(3+2\\text{i})z-(3-2\\text{i})z+(3+2\\text{i})(3-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(-2)({z}^{2}-(3+2\\text{i}+3-2\\text{i})z+({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2})) \\\\  & =(-2)({z}^{2}-6z+(9+4)) \\\\  & =-2{z}^{2}+12z-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=-2{z}^{2}+12z-26.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70c149d8-3739-564c-97c5-6636860ad0c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given Two Imaginary or Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f2712e-bbf9-5d64-b4a4-19fa66e817ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}-12v+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327527} {:answer/id #uuid "7dea58a2-f43b-55a7-9363-8cbf0140d0fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}-6v+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328021} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c61ffe-0e5e-5090-911a-89611958a46f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}+8v-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328382} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5b7e26-c574-59a3-b54b-9724ee8d1044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}-16v-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329017} {:answer/id #uuid "ff80448e-3917-589d-a32d-f1e60b6bfd66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}+6v+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43f2712e-bbf9-5d64-b4a4-19fa66e817ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{v}^{2}-12v+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327527} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a83e02-7c9e-5529-ab3d-121fdc72ecd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af81e241-3fd9-5579-878e-052d0c216987" :question/math-academy-id "q-46669" :question/problem "The quadratic polynomial $f(v)$ has real coefficients, has roots ${v}_{1}=3+4\\text{i}$ and ${v}_{2}=3-4\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $2.$ Find $f(v).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${v}_{1}=3+4\\text{i}$ and ${v}_{2}=3-4\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(v-{v}_{1})(v-{v}_{2}) & =2(v-(3+4\\text{i}))(v-(3-4\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(v-(3+4\\text{i}))(v-(3-4\\text{i})) & =2({v}^{2}-(3+4\\text{i})v-(3-4\\text{i})v+(3+4\\text{i})(3-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2({v}^{2}-(3+4\\text{i}+3-4\\text{i})v+({3}^{2}-{4}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2})) \\\\  & =2({v}^{2}-6v+(9+16)) \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-12v+50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(v)=2{v}^{2}-12v+50.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0801cf1c-5fd2-5cc2-8874-bb033ff63b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326999} {:answer/id #uuid "21618e1a-d4b1-5642-ac4b-4adec6bcb616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327222} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3ed060-8f3d-5e5f-a548-ad4adb40f6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327301} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5696e8-74ce-57d4-98a1-81edbd390bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327489} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d71e6-17cd-53a0-9ad8-987841eae685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a3ed060-8f3d-5e5f-a548-ad4adb40f6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327301} :answer-field/id #uuid "914d0201-289f-570f-b5ca-55e4260ae1f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "005f9ab9-467f-5576-9116-815c97776952" :question/math-academy-id "q-46830" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=4$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{(\\sqrt{3x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{{(\\sqrt{3x-3})}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-3-9} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3(x-4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+3}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3b50a7-1a31-52bc-91a4-f6b268955f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327070} {:answer/id #uuid "10710463-0db1-5838-be7b-4f17aca898c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327081} {:answer/id #uuid "196dae27-2242-5069-9416-9d2435e009f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327154} {:answer/id #uuid "519f1ddd-7a1e-5d1a-a24e-d9445baa5f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327645} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2ff551-9a15-5bb4-987f-b343fdf4c92b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519f1ddd-7a1e-5d1a-a24e-d9445baa5f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327645} :answer-field/id #uuid "3556b158-4519-5e67-be7a-f8d92924db68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a2f35e-6a3d-5f89-a27d-35edfe302559" :question/math-academy-id "q-46831" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3552e823-ed7e-5411-8a4d-a7a161d920bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327395} {:answer/id #uuid "3f22fb2d-15a6-595d-8e20-54b09e482167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327487} {:answer/id #uuid "6874f84d-9c2d-5c6c-8e62-74ddd0d5cfc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327835} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8ddd5a-8d1e-5b11-a6bc-864708f334f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327996} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89a2bc-ead2-5238-9aae-15a92a90fa81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f22fb2d-15a6-595d-8e20-54b09e482167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327487} :answer-field/id #uuid "10bd8af9-359c-5678-a39c-41fc93bcc176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5898d1b3-7352-5733-948f-e0e93cd0becf" :question/math-academy-id "q-47039" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9d0e9d944cda9c97555ffdeac5aa245905f800cd99e11f5d172203bad3892d.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17028eac-4ec5-5412-a437-179e2341db73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327136} {:answer/id #uuid "699925f0-6e8c-5f1a-b77d-e1c6f323fb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327842} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0370f2-56b3-5cc8-a52d-9b4a98865995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328406} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe3a0e-970e-5bb9-8a35-e018230647f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745} {:answer/id #uuid "f5621511-1f8d-5799-9acc-c62ea19974de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe3a0e-970e-5bb9-8a35-e018230647f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f990210-b8a8-5e34-9486-0ad7ae987df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66cb54a9-7be0-554a-a6fe-eb1aaa1b6f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-47073" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x-8}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}\\cdot {2}^{-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({2}^{3})}^{x}\\cdot {2}^{-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={8}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/85/85319605a7c7b57d4c7ed29b8d409e3169f3331775580b91b70e2be99fef7986.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}=(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ff46e30-81e1-52bf-b55f-724406f58c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398836} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6c153e-3504-5702-bfa8-a1175ee38323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398837} {:answer/id #uuid "586fee85-5bd1-5bd9-b903-3f988c962fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398838} {:answer/id #uuid "89bd78d0-e4f8-53a4-8ab8-3044e809bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398839} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8bb4af-dfd9-5eef-baf3-2724ee4fd9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff46e30-81e1-52bf-b55f-724406f58c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398836} :answer-field/id #uuid "b49fc3fc-44cb-5db2-9fc5-ed9e471c021f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-47077" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3cb48f6-7226-52f0-b6e9-cc9be278a789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398843} {:answer/id #uuid "b3570634-e445-5816-bd14-931c93860185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398844} {:answer/id #uuid "af392d2a-05d0-5dd2-abb7-493478be4513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398845} {:answer/id #uuid "958e4525-323d-5584-adaa-91a9e8036aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398846} {:answer/id #uuid "ae811028-5105-53e1-bc94-6774288d6c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3570634-e445-5816-bd14-931c93860185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa48aaf-f754-52f3-a0c5-5c38dac2c7d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f05bf34-eff5-5d2f-b84c-b025710dd1a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47101" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)=(x-2)(x+2)(x-5).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $g(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[2,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4]$, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $g(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $g(2)$ and $g(4).$\n\nSince $g(2)=0$ and $g(4)=-12,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-12,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-12,0].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d4ce3e-e14e-5bcf-bfc9-14d42ec6961a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327133} {:answer/id #uuid "1b93c55a-83df-5d0e-bdec-bf25395077d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327173} {:answer/id #uuid "6b07c70b-b235-5e11-ad5b-c604150c191c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327856} {:answer/id #uuid "88d21fb1-2bba-55bb-9d28-4dbf210e7501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328110} {:answer/id #uuid "d12ce287-32a7-53b1-8099-7b4bbe7a15b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d4ce3e-e14e-5bcf-bfc9-14d42ec6961a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327133} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab49ca4-40c8-5931-ad5c-10529867134f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7e04a47-4e01-5d83-814c-8ea3ea72f529" :question/math-academy-id "q-4726" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60191a14-5ad2-55ab-8217-e91fe73325eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327778} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5449f6-af3e-5d64-928d-75f1cc3ae7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328014} {:answer/id #uuid "a4614944-9ea5-5d63-9245-8473caa7f386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328356} {:answer/id #uuid "d16e7253-9c7c-5f19-8b69-51e227595eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328775} {:answer/id #uuid "edfe6469-630a-5f72-bd88-748a646f1d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4614944-9ea5-5d63-9245-8473caa7f386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328356} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6e4de6-e749-5260-9e23-6206beb6fb02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "995f3b18-ad68-56fb-a94d-9e7f0a50f6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-47367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e651cb4-ae24-5ac7-9c01-d90de562b155" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fdde19a-9963-5314-a730-38d3e7e7d77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328036} {:answer/id #uuid "97a2ee61-e6dc-5e36-81f5-fc3db982586a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328247} {:answer/id #uuid "9a37ff06-0b54-5882-9413-033df94614ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328268} {:answer/id #uuid "c7871896-4fe7-5a6b-8eb8-191696a5ea9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328686} {:answer/id #uuid "e7380d07-906d-5376-8094-784ef1882e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fdde19a-9963-5314-a730-38d3e7e7d77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328036} :answer-field/id #uuid "90e4783b-c8c6-5ab9-a5f3-c8bf38c87745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dc02dcb-7752-56a6-a0db-14303fb52f4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47378" :question/problem "Andrey wants to estimate his running speed. Andrey's friend Max takes note of Andrey's position while he is on the track. Max's observations are given in the table below, where $s(t)$ is the distance covered, measured in meters, $t$ seconds after Andrey started running.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $9$ | $15$ | $20$ | $27$ |\n\nApproximate Andrey's speed at the point where $t=6$ seconds using a forward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t+h)-s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{s(6+2)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(8)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=6$ Andrey's speed is approximately $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092389 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "167f04f0-c08e-51d8-acfa-2c4660083e6a" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb3d392-20cc-50f7-8ed4-08fd6e7422f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327060} {:answer/id #uuid "8028f5c1-e970-5397-966f-1d78458d0754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.5\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328038} {:answer/id #uuid "a398c98c-641a-5b90-bcd7-b2554208fe1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328351} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f3eea0-c8fc-562e-aee3-ab18455ba6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328652} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b3e6dc-89e2-503e-832e-13cfaf7180f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f3eea0-c8fc-562e-aee3-ab18455ba6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328652} :answer-field/id #uuid "22a7f30d-1243-5d26-93b2-b8a922ca3b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2e860e6-e569-50bb-bb89-eb16bb320f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47383" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a7e89a828cbd660357d4834e0bcb9cb5c316dbe24419865206ba5f41872963d.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t),$ where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above. \nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{W(t+h)-W(t)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{W(1+2)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{W(3)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100-50}{2} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ minute, the power is increasing at a rate of approximately $25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "419134c3-3177-5d7d-86f6-899a73338b1c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e53716-4824-540d-910f-e96de9e7705c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327125} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6829e3-25a2-5ca9-905a-56092bc2c2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327582} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3d1492-7305-5935-bb31-396e2019b697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327591} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb9f8b1-f7c6-55ca-bc7e-2d9764cbc868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327768} {:answer/id #uuid "d21a325f-372e-5b34-953b-a0f5bca1ac99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e53716-4824-540d-910f-e96de9e7705c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327125} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5c3ea1-22a5-5ac4-8885-7499bdfc84e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2c5a5b5-434b-5353-b9f5-c6b55039b842" :question/math-academy-id "q-47387" :question/problem "![](images/00/000419fa9eb4e18b5b236ef63eeb419403a0882fe87debfab8802ad7295de125.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2+4)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10131252-4695-56ed-8c59-747e545f594c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392584} {:answer/id #uuid "e6984861-e3cb-5365-9e9e-1ecd4311dcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392585} {:answer/id #uuid "859687f9-c59b-551f-aaa5-e7e3c04ba6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392586} {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} {:answer/id #uuid "41016988-d005-506a-b687-2940e1c88cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9549a2f-75a6-5fa7-b3e3-19c27b3b484f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "414ee72f-747c-573a-9cad-a9cbdad6101a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47410" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(7) & =-\\frac{1}{10}(x-1) \\\\ 10y-70 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+10y & =71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=10+71=81.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a6c80-4207-50f6-8a34-1d93a5f89fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327287} {:answer/id #uuid "307e57c3-1572-5e33-a5f9-3472b90bf91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327363} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ee7da7-a08a-5f34-86ea-f3f106a5f98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328912} {:answer/id #uuid "e33c1589-993a-5e15-9743-78a5a9f95009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328926} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c839b-8755-5fd8-b8d8-b4929a06d14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c839b-8755-5fd8-b8d8-b4929a06d14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142} :answer-field/id #uuid "197f2e71-4c00-5a81-830b-269b6b29fd90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "704dafc6-9839-55e3-9683-dc5e275270d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47449" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029e14a6-a3da-5d56-bd50-24aa3841ff59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326964} {:answer/id #uuid "09838c12-324b-5f86-b8eb-b9a50a5b99f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327012} {:answer/id #uuid "58d2b82d-ca43-5016-b810-dc807085c710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327713} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fe1f38-597e-53c5-aa82-041838d9bba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328838} {:answer/id #uuid "ed61f5fa-bf44-559a-b259-d059d2ed706f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed61f5fa-bf44-559a-b259-d059d2ed706f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329011} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0b4dd2-5359-5754-b6be-a086a2742846" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce070dc-f77b-529a-a508-4630f3613570" :question/math-academy-id "q-47455" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n- The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n- The function has no zeros." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has no zeros."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb5ae7b-9517-5392-88a9-3feafc851898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327463} {:answer/id #uuid "7184ccc4-6242-5943-99e3-8ed3f28981d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327913} {:answer/id #uuid "b51ac9db-8526-580b-a2b9-18769522f3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.015\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328509} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3ac3f5-6514-5dbc-84a8-d5e2a8faf18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328756} {:answer/id #uuid "f482d568-d49c-5121-8e5f-e465706589b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7184ccc4-6242-5943-99e3-8ed3f28981d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327913} :answer-field/id #uuid "01273c83-0e6f-5cee-9b71-aff4fc11a7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb7f1f5d-3970-5822-8a66-2e4c6c6b1ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47485" :question/problem "A baseball is hit by a batter. The distance between the batter and the ball, measured in miles, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $0.015$ | $0.03$ | $0.045$ | $0.05$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ when $t=1$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{s(1)-s(1-0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1)-s(0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.03-0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is $0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0047254f-2b32-51db-9ed2-928c6f1e9eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${20}^{∘}$ per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326936} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef791e9-c211-57ba-9b0e-a972470cd03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${4}^{∘}$ per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327066} {:answer/id #uuid "b909dcb4-5592-5fe1-aaae-707c991efeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${16}^{∘}$ per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328548} {:answer/id #uuid "d70907c8-d0bd-5231-8d30-2069e238714f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${8}^{∘}$ per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328817} {:answer/id #uuid "d791554c-7561-5b77-bef2-3454474aa46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${40}^{∘}$ per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d791554c-7561-5b77-bef2-3454474aa46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${40}^{∘}$ per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328823} :answer-field/id #uuid "3219062c-8ac5-5b3b-8591-17f30da48131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3963ea08-d6d3-51e8-ab54-1dde883d3239" :question/math-academy-id "q-47504" :question/problem "The temperature of a certain liquid under heating is given by the function $T(t),$ where $t$ is the time in hours. The values of $T(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ | $2.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $-12$ | $-10$ | $-4$ | $6$ | $10$ | $13$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nDetermine the instantaneous temperature rate at the instant of time $t=1.6$ using a central difference approximation with the step size $h=0.2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{T}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{T(t+h)-T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1.6$ with step size $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(1.6) & ≈\\frac{T(1.6+0.2)-T(1.6-0.2)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(1.8)-T(1.4)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-(-10)}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{0.4} \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous temperature rate at the instant of time $t=1.6$ is ${40}^{∘}$ per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa7b587-c0dd-5f75-99d1-6272f92bfdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327028} {:answer/id #uuid "753d7428-d52e-5596-ae3a-ff64246a9df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327941} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5de371-b851-5d4d-a6f5-a69835b1e005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328167} {:answer/id #uuid "a1aca8c0-01bd-51d4-b46a-b5a9f074c361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328338} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5fe981-b2e7-5a2d-b8c1-709d5dba3592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "753d7428-d52e-5596-ae3a-ff64246a9df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327941} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3e751f-17fa-5e28-8951-b6fad2158d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "102c3407-88cf-50ee-a99d-4011f1f2d634" :question/math-academy-id "q-47552" :question/problem "![](images/34/34145d4a924027964096df2eae6e87d31832833528aef06203a1b1f43f73d2ac.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=4$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2)-f(2-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814a3015-040d-5d38-8d89-66e613ea9c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$, $x = 0$, and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114236} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc4ca4a-1ffc-505e-9c15-eada8368fb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114237} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f356c3-5dd2-587a-aa58-7ab4bc07f2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114238} {:answer/id #uuid "cd84e69a-febf-59d0-8ce5-b1b9373d8954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114239} {:answer/id #uuid "551564d0-36a1-5e6f-a7f1-ebd7c47f4f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114240}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3caeafb3-6b65-5977-a595-ae7d734d711e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7c45a8-6d0f-5a1d-b8dc-4e8e1b4b6ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396662} {:answer/id #uuid "f7475fa2-2dfb-5ab6-af30-4b6beda786d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396663} {:answer/id #uuid "1541152a-7611-5c14-a588-d1e5cc5e22df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396664} {:answer/id #uuid "453cb038-db89-5863-98ef-ba1dcdd04c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396665} {:answer/id #uuid "edc154f2-6797-5d5a-be28-0f6a1f59d668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1541152a-7611-5c14-a588-d1e5cc5e22df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396664} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e2164a-d23d-528c-85fa-147024e56737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "097f3e11-2ce8-55f7-bbfa-1640ee282b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-47650" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1891a184-adbe-5f7c-b5e9-88a6e9bf190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327146} {:answer/id #uuid "36fc7ba9-3e3d-5355-b5a4-955b062950a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327412} {:answer/id #uuid "5195185b-66e6-5f1a-9118-15cd44f5a8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327643} {:answer/id #uuid "531028f9-ce96-5ffe-a8a9-62fff3ebb31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327655} {:answer/id #uuid "d67f7b2c-c5cb-5679-b7d3-c4437cfebd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36fc7ba9-3e3d-5355-b5a4-955b062950a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327412} :answer-field/id #uuid "221c4059-f483-5fb5-989e-d4a381f46cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5470569-5538-5c85-aec6-dfcb3a6e366a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47652" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution\n- The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf6e8497-4669-5084-9732-38c2fbe4d251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf9beb1c95dc2d69b430a47be217b98b81bec65c3370099397ab70672f8cbe54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114248} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f2438d-6d60-5115-af40-26ada984c0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1ef7e9ce0d1db1e60721d5f44b094d23091fd42b02b3b93832f4b17523241c87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114249} {:answer/id #uuid "36d333d5-7a67-54ef-91f8-131a778f0daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf943c2d6f9281c62f82712485bce8ab06b7312e8156baf0b961e858e0f28ea6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114250} {:answer/id #uuid "4d064a0e-8c94-5a25-be42-711ce4c45961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e9b1f8848e09eb936116d39dc72b9d15bded2426b2e4de23f88e257cbe9d46f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114251} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae424b3-cbc6-57db-ad88-a9fc93750070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d297db86c650af71f2d116f65470aa46445aceede8f81462e22e1becb284736a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114252}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0c90a9f6-9f64-5438-bbf5-7a3d896f8873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114247} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36bac047-eabf-53d8-9e97-1e6ace55c083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf9beb1c95dc2d69b430a47be217b98b81bec65c3370099397ab70672f8cbe54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392590} {:answer/id #uuid "04671946-230b-5e70-8f2c-ff566e8b3b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d297db86c650af71f2d116f65470aa46445aceede8f81462e22e1becb284736a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392591} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e33187-d6c4-5fbc-b100-c663848334e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf943c2d6f9281c62f82712485bce8ab06b7312e8156baf0b961e858e0f28ea6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392592} {:answer/id #uuid "218adc50-a59c-5ded-a9cc-466c8124d139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1ef7e9ce0d1db1e60721d5f44b094d23091fd42b02b3b93832f4b17523241c87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392593} {:answer/id #uuid "8bee35bf-0fbc-59b3-ad02-d3fba93e60a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e9b1f8848e09eb936116d39dc72b9d15bded2426b2e4de23f88e257cbe9d46f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04671946-230b-5e70-8f2c-ff566e8b3b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d297db86c650af71f2d116f65470aa46445aceede8f81462e22e1becb284736a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392591} :answer-field/id #uuid "36fddb3c-5df5-5efe-9d09-31fdd773672d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2d358ee-4f00-5039-8abe-a47808bd6f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-47669" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ \nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=h(x).$\n\nThe other graphs do not have a vertical tangent inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The functions $y=p(x)$ and $y=q(x)$ are not defined at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf7ce1a-6058-5910-9e37-9c52f5803f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327752} {:answer/id #uuid "83d1af3e-3afd-5d91-88f4-3ed406eb55ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328073} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bc5f0c-c5c4-5da0-8587-7d4be22ee1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328514} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fe587a-30bf-504d-8f35-c395e902bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328961} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ae9dbf-d366-51c4-8592-d07406f566fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7fe587a-30bf-504d-8f35-c395e902bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328961} :answer-field/id #uuid "06aff75e-c521-5c5d-91ff-ff642e60a2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f90e0f73-a650-56a2-851e-2b406e202da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47679" :question/problem "Given that that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$, and  is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x-1|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ac00fa-c573-50b2-9181-c5c47c436adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327214} {:answer/id #uuid "6664d395-2cd4-57fa-8267-dad7b7f6653c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327822} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7699cf-1310-5e2c-8483-50775b4a0f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328441} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d74407-8722-5f57-a238-174a8da68bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328700} {:answer/id #uuid "cf91c4ec-315b-5bb2-ba9b-e695b2ead9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6664d395-2cd4-57fa-8267-dad7b7f6653c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327822} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e41401-65d6-5b8b-a09b-c7db46fa5956" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8298d153-fa1d-58c6-bfcd-efab6041f9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47681" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nI. It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n\nII. It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement I is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5525d2c5-8da5-5b7a-9dec-548bb1f64862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327670} {:answer/id #uuid "d32113f6-8db1-51c9-b343-962d9eecf5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328787} {:answer/id #uuid "db68c9ef-ce55-599c-bc00-27824736bb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328850} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd7c213-368f-5fb0-8c96-c6383043d8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328895} {:answer/id #uuid "e7294816-d07d-53cb-8486-58993c4119bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5525d2c5-8da5-5b7a-9dec-548bb1f64862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327670} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe19b9b9-08c6-5cb6-ad38-8ea58562c3f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0beb9b2-97ad-5a80-853a-12a09696e0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-4771" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-10t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-10t-2) \\\\  & =2t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-10 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =10 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a6c5f1-fdda-51a7-9af5-55a7988c6ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327213} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7bf23a-3b51-50b3-b4e9-f2d073f69b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327325} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc51e81-041a-5be8-90cd-546051875d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327361} {:answer/id #uuid "574a128a-4326-5a1b-8f72-cb7781cce5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327693} {:answer/id #uuid "d933b8b3-9c7e-58ef-882a-0b78b4a59d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc51e81-041a-5be8-90cd-546051875d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327361} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6ba5c8-31d8-5c13-87be-35c5a1f633f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ee96606-8ba8-569e-8f90-21918ad24c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-47787" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a0fc8a-86ac-5ab6-acd1-3ec0072b7256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327288} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd35f1-1cf5-5986-8bcd-cae0e1e256a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328506} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a685ba-076b-591a-b200-e9ee6e74f0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328951} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f4c678-2ef4-5109-bc2c-e164d61c301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329047} {:answer/id #uuid "f598a74d-ecb1-5d47-9ac6-e6464cddea05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no vertical asymptotes." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd35f1-1cf5-5986-8bcd-cae0e1e256a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328506} :answer-field/id #uuid "5859fa75-27d1-5a4e-9275-4eb9c181d62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2ba45bb-fcdc-536e-a19c-0143c96613a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47854" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{5}{x-\\sqrt{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "There are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-\\sqrt{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/09/09c19a6049d77a2b57c093ca714627a453c99bf52af27ae656ad1f4e779d1866.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c7c09f-3771-5ac2-8492-cb564408d161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326995} {:answer/id #uuid "09a6f667-741d-5bd1-8b8a-e0169fa9064e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327016} {:answer/id #uuid "5b54c3bd-e1c0-5f6e-949c-a3321c243ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327735} {:answer/id #uuid "b402004a-0bc7-5a2a-89cb-61969dcaee78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328497} {:answer/id #uuid "b930f089-e00a-5693-9851-95f660218c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b930f089-e00a-5693-9851-95f660218c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328551} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a34845-2a7f-510c-bb16-9154a49b42a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d67546-ee63-5a73-9d7b-983a73ffa720" :question/math-academy-id "q-4790" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6390a69-73ca-5747-ad35-6ad1cc3e50ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402904} {:answer/id #uuid "bf810803-9256-56b1-b55f-ffc26fa5e427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402905} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3e8609-ca99-5684-877b-a0d89799e552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402906} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c87f0b-5047-5a28-9f98-5daf036d20f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402907} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca02ff7-1af5-56d8-b08a-9a759e9da705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf810803-9256-56b1-b55f-ffc26fa5e427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402905} :answer-field/id #uuid "e587e32b-f21e-545a-8ef6-e7a910ea785c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "126dc5db-7aa0-5e31-885e-35517971a655" :question/math-academy-id "q-47918" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a1843255ea718116bd2daa07f1375a3fb277c47ec8503fec90da824cd714d0b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,10],$ which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest point is $D,$ and so this point represents the global maximum of $f(x).$ \n\n**Note:** The point $B$ is not a global maximum, it's a local maximum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af784ce-3cef-5945-8932-b03fe43316e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402911} {:answer/id #uuid "9673d400-5f29-5ce5-b5e9-8047de8eb008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402912} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5c6830-b8b8-54b9-9b05-279cc0d24408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402913} {:answer/id #uuid "f088b713-a43a-50e7-9574-07ac077ab3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402914} {:answer/id #uuid "62bbee0f-e18c-5110-a2d4-144a718f657e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9673d400-5f29-5ce5-b5e9-8047de8eb008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402912} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f254e3-3027-52e3-8cb7-edd707cacbbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62b39803-cf8b-5f2a-91f9-b83f48f12d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47919" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9d8fb6d663d8dfd0cce24ae3817c936e2e97ab2df7aefeaa0c734caf0311b94.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,10]$, which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest points are $B$ and $D$, and so these points represents the global maximum of $f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24cdb15d-92a4-57bb-b3ae-70ee6398efed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}sin⁡x-cos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327252} {:answer/id #uuid "28cffc72-f500-56eb-b62d-297789e3d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327280} {:answer/id #uuid "537f4f22-dc76-5ecc-941c-28735a7fbb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}sin⁡x-cos⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327658} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb04a5b-8198-547f-9f85-6d03684ff103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(sin⁡x-cos⁡x)}{cos⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327873} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebf154b-78ed-5323-9bc6-5af4e4ce0e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(sin⁡x-cos⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cffc72-f500-56eb-b62d-297789e3d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1139e71c-df1c-5f00-8bad-e71886ea7ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ace0fb1-1a71-5cfd-952c-d11857b972c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-47943" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{cos⁡x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′}}{(cos⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}cos⁡x+{e}^{x}sin⁡x}{(cos⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355f05a8-1b69-5fc8-be47-894b11d7bfab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327397} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe28f7-f30d-502c-86da-651e061a7de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328090} {:answer/id #uuid "aea5ab3d-2e57-5f46-9645-4bae9723adb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328450} {:answer/id #uuid "c56f6ed4-9bb4-5cc5-b67c-a8b61a3ad0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328663} {:answer/id #uuid "e88d667c-7ec1-53b1-becf-5cea6607291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e88d667c-7ec1-53b1-becf-5cea6607291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328968} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e646dc1-b4b3-54e3-afbb-f460a293900c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77fb0fc1-711e-5505-92d1-e385391bf5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-4798" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+5 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026a56b8-1115-5684-ac6e-ef1630e64970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326960} {:answer/id #uuid "0393bd6c-c00c-5794-bb05-1c8299be09b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{sec}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326972} {:answer/id #uuid "25ff194a-cfcd-58a0-adba-8bc91fdf1efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327262} {:answer/id #uuid "44d191be-38a4-500e-8cfb-76a9d83fe2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}sec⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327534} {:answer/id #uuid "9336f84c-bb60-54b1-ba79-495700d341f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9336f84c-bb60-54b1-ba79-495700d341f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328218} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c98579-7ff9-5677-8870-c03daabb35df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db3973ed-197d-5377-b8db-60e027e16ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47983" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\sqrt{x}+tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+tan⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+{sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
